From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] shell32: a program that needs an administrator, opened from the desktop, asks at once (8701)

sg_execute_via_broker waits up to 180 s for the broker's answer (the
SgElevate rundll32 sets the ready event) in the thread that called
ShellExecuteEx. When that thread is a window's -- Explorer's desktop on a
double-click of a requireAdministrator installer -- it took no message
meanwhile. A new process's start sends to the desktop's window, so rundll32
SgElevate, and every other program started then, stood still until the
wait gave up: the consent prompt came after exactly three minutes
(LibreCAD's installer on qa4). The wait now delivers the messages other
threads send to this one (MsgWaitForMultipleObjectsEx with QS_SENDMESSAGE,
PeekMessage PM_QS_SENDMESSAGE); posted and input messages stay queued, the
180 s limit, the broker handshake and the consent are unchanged. Mutant
SG_MUTANT_WAIT_WITHOUT_SENT_MESSAGES.
---
--- a/dlls/shell32/shlexec.c
+++ b/dlls/shell32/shlexec.c
@@ -459,6 +459,29 @@
  * --wine tells sg-elevate the rest is a Windows command to run under Wine as
  * SYSTEM. Only with the client installed, and never inside a program the
  * broker itself launched (SG_IN_BROKER). */
+/* Wait for one of HANDLES as long as TIMEOUT, delivering the messages other
+ * threads send to this one meanwhile (8701). The thread asking is often a
+ * window's: Explorer's desktop opening a program that needs an administrator
+ * on a double-click. A new process's start sends to the desktop's window, so
+ * rundll32 (and every other program started then) stood still until this
+ * wait gave up: the consent prompt came after three minutes. Posted and input
+ * messages stay queued, as when a Windows UI thread waits in
+ * MsgWaitForMultipleObjects for sent messages only. */
+static void sg_wait_serving_sent( const HANDLE *handles, DWORD count, DWORD timeout )
+{
+    DWORD start = GetTickCount(), elapsed, ret;
+    MSG msg;
+
+    for (;;)
+    {
+        elapsed = GetTickCount() - start;
+        if (elapsed >= timeout) return;
+        ret = MsgWaitForMultipleObjectsEx( count, handles, timeout - elapsed, QS_SENDMESSAGE, 0 );
+        if (ret != WAIT_OBJECT_0 + count) return;
+        PeekMessageW( &msg, NULL, 0, 0, PM_NOREMOVE | PM_QS_SENDMESSAGE );
+    }
+}
+
 static BOOL sg_execute_via_broker( const WCHAR *lpCmd, DWORD flags, void *env,
                                    STARTUPINFOW *startup, PROCESS_INFORMATION *info, BOOL *declined )
 {
@@ -516,7 +539,11 @@
         /* the prompt waits two minutes for an answer; then the launch */
         HANDLE handles[2] = { ready, info->hProcess };
         DWORD code;
+#ifdef SG_MUTANT_WAIT_WITHOUT_SENT_MESSAGES
         WaitForMultipleObjects( 2, handles, FALSE, 180000 );
+#else
+        sg_wait_serving_sent( handles, 2, 180000 );
+#endif
         /* refused: SgElevate ends with ERROR_CANCELLED straight after the
          * answer; ShellExecuteEx fails with it, as Windows' does when the
          * consent is not given */
